# syntax -- the shell grammar learn explains alongside a command's manpage.
#
# when you hit tab (or `man`) on a question, the panel shows the command's ref
# AND every shell operator, special variable and expansion your answer uses --
# the pieces that are not a command and so have no manpage of their own, but
# that you still have to know to build the line.
#
# three tab-separated fields: label, an ERE that detects it, a one-line gloss.
# ROW ORDER IS CONSUMED ORDER: the detector removes each match before testing
# the rows below, so the long forms ($((, 2>&1, ||, ${#, /dev/null) are ruled
# on before the short ones they contain. a match is replaced with a single
# space, not deleted outright -- deleting would fuse the tokens on either side
# of it, and a later boundary-sensitive row (--, a lone -) would then read
# them as one token and miss. only constructs busybox ash actually runs are
# listed -- no <(cmd), no <<<, no ${v//x/y}, no arrays. quoting (' " \) is
# handled in explain(), not here -- see the comment there.
/dev/null	[0-9]?>>?/dev/null	the bit bucket -- writes are discarded, reads give nothing
$(( ))	\$\(\(	evaluate arithmetic: $((2 + 3)) becomes 5
2>&1	2>&1	send errors (fd 2) wherever stdout is pointed right now
>&2	>&[0-9]	send stdout to the error channel -- echo x >&2 prints to stderr
2>	2>	redirect errors (fd 2) to a file
>>	>>	append stdout to a file, keeping what is there
>	>	redirect stdout to a file -- creates it, or wipes what is there
<<	<<	heredoc: everything up to the end marker becomes stdin
<	<	read stdin from a file instead of the keyboard
{}	\{\}	in find -exec, {} stands for each file found
\;	\\;	\; runs the command once per file; + batches them into one
[ ]	\[ 	test a condition -- [ is a command, so the spaces are required
||	\|\|	run the next command only if this one FAILED (nonzero exit)
&&	&&	run the next command only if this one SUCCEEDED (zero exit)
|	\|	pipe: this command's stdout becomes the next one's stdin
;	;	run one command then the next, no matter what
--	(^|[[:space:]])--([[:space:]]|$)	end of options: a later -x is an argument, not a flag
NAME=	^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*=	NAME=VALUE sets a variable, no spaces around the =
$?	\$\?	exit status of the last command -- 0 means success
$$	\$\$	the process id of this shell
$!	\$!	the process id of the last command started with &
$#	\$#	how many positional parameters ($1, $2, ...) there are
$@	\$@	every positional parameter -- "$@" keeps each as its own word
$*	\$\*	every positional parameter -- "$*" joins them into one word
$0	\$0	the name the script or shell was invoked as
$1..$9	\$[1-9]	a positional parameter -- $1 is the first argument
${#NAME}	\$\{#	the length of a variable in characters: ${#NAME}
${NAME:-WORD}	\$\{[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*:-	a variable value, or WORD if it is unset/empty: ${NAME:-WORD}
${NAME#%}	\$\{[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*[#%]	strip a prefix (#) or suffix (%) off a variable: ${NAME%.log}
${NAME}	\$\{	expand a variable; braces let you glue text right after it
$(CMD)	\$\(	command substitution: run a command and become its output
`CMD`	`	command substitution, the older form of $(...)
$NAME	\$[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*	expand a variable value -- unquoted, the result also word-splits
!	!	negate: run the command, then flip its success and failure
( )	\(	runs the commands in a subshell -- changes inside do not survive
{ }	\{ 	groups commands in this shell -- keep the spaces, and the last ;
&	&	run the command in the background and carry on
[...]	\[[^] ]*\]	one character from the set, e.g. [0-9] -- glob and regex alike
*	\*	a filename glob the shell expands before ls ever runs: 0+ characters, never a leading dot. in a grep/sed/awk pattern: 0+ of what precedes it
?	\?	also expanded by the shell: exactly one character of a filename. in a grep -E pattern: 0 or 1 of what precedes it
~	~	your home directory -- the shell swaps it in, not the command
-	(^|[[:space:]])-([[:space:]]|$)	a lone - means read stdin, by convention
